WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.832
Welcome to the Tueri Systems Risk Brief for Monday, October twenty-sixth.

2
00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:07.604
Operational clarity when conditions change.

3
00:00:07.604 --> 00:00:12.208
A few minutes on what is happening, what can be confirmed, and what has changed.

4
00:00:12.208 --> 00:00:13.667
Digitally narrated.

5
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:19.331
A tanker was disabled in the Strait of Hormuz on Friday, in an attack only reported this morning.

6
00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:23.172
The Houthis claim a new missile attack on Riyadh's main airport.

7
00:00:23.172 --> 00:00:26.497
A strong cyclone is heading for India's east coast.

8
00:00:26.497 --> 00:00:29.221
And Uganda has tightened its borders over Ebola.

9
00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:31.805
Here's the detail.

10
00:00:31.805 --> 00:00:32.733
Shipping first.

11
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:43.861
Confirmed: the UK Maritime Trade Operations center says it has a verified, late report that on Friday an outbound tanker in the Strait of Hormuz was struck by an unknown projectile and disabled.

12
00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:45.692
The crew are reported safe.

13
00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:48.586
There is no damage or environmental assessment yet.

14
00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:52.985
This is a separate incident from the tanker hit on Thursday, which kept sailing.

15
00:00:52.985 --> 00:01:00.346
It means the latest multinational maritime update, which counted one attack in the strait for that period, is now out of date.

16
00:01:00.346 --> 00:01:06.303
That update rates the threat in the strait as severe, and traffic remains well below last year's levels.

17
00:01:06.303 --> 00:01:12.794
For anyone with cargo or crew moving through the strait, the practical point is that attacks can surface days late.

18
00:01:12.794 --> 00:01:19.353
A few quiet days of reporting is not the same as a few quiet days at sea.

19
00:01:19.353 --> 00:01:19.853
Riyadh.

20
00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:30.081
Claim only: the Houthis say they fired a ballistic missile at King Khalid International Airport early Monday, and drones at pumping stations on Saudi Arabia's East-West oil pipeline.

21
00:01:30.081 --> 00:01:36.882
Reported: the Saudi-led coalition says a missile headed toward Riyadh was intercepted, and that it destroyed two launchers in Sanaa.

22
00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:40.206
Saudi civil defense issued a danger alert for Riyadh.

23
00:01:40.206 --> 00:01:44.494
There is no Saudi confirmation of any damage.

24
00:01:44.494 --> 00:01:48.476
At the airport itself, Flightradar twenty-four data at about five forty a.m.

25
00:01:48.476 --> 00:01:58.032
Central, early afternoon in Riyadh, showed seventy-eight cancellations so far today, about nineteen percent of roughly four hundred twenty scheduled flights, with little delay.

26
00:01:58.032 --> 00:02:01.738
On Sunday afternoon it showed about fifty-nine percent cancelled.

27
00:02:01.738 --> 00:02:07.273
That is a partial reopening with less disruption so far today, not a full return of service.

28
00:02:07.273 --> 00:02:09.459
Government guidance has not loosened.

29
00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:18.578
Australia's Smartraveller said Sunday evening: do not travel to Saudi Arabia, and do not travel to the airports in Riyadh, Dammam or Abha.

30
00:02:18.578 --> 00:02:21.918
Britain still advises staying away from the Riyadh airport.

31
00:02:21.918 --> 00:02:29.055
The US advisory remains Level three, reconsider travel, with non-emergency staff ordered out since Friday.

32
00:02:29.055 --> 00:02:36.376
Saudi, Turkish and Pakistani officials are reported to be meeting Iranian officials in Tehran today about de-escalation.

33
00:02:36.376 --> 00:02:37.292
India.

34
00:02:37.292 --> 00:02:52.019
Reported: India's weather department expects Cyclone Arnab to cross the coast of Andhra Pradesh by Wednesday morning local time as a very severe cyclonic storm, with sustained winds of about one hundred fifteen to one hundred twenty-five kilometers an hour and gusts to about one hundred forty.

35
00:02:52.019 --> 00:03:02.508
Andhra Pradesh says about five hundred seventy thousand people in vulnerable areas are being moved starting this evening, and fishing boats have been called in.

36
00:03:02.508 --> 00:03:06.914
A neighboring state has closed schools in five districts on Tuesday and Wednesday.

37
00:03:06.914 --> 00:03:14.332
Organizations with staff or suppliers on that coast should review movement plans for Tuesday through Thursday.

38
00:03:14.332 --> 00:03:15.308
Uganda.

39
00:03:15.308 --> 00:03:27.808
Confirmed: Uganda's health ministry says that, because of the Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, travelers who have been in Congo are no longer allowed to transit Uganda by any means, including charter flights.

40
00:03:27.808 --> 00:03:32.788
Ugandans returning from Congo face twenty-one days of quarantine at their own cost.

41
00:03:32.788 --> 00:03:40.674
Non-essential Congolese travelers are being turned back, and essential travelers such as humanitarian workers and truck drivers are being screened.

42
00:03:40.674 --> 00:03:44.502
Screening has been stepped up at the main airport and at land borders.

43
00:03:44.502 --> 00:03:51.247
If your teams route into eastern Congo through Uganda, those plans need checking today.

44
00:03:51.247 --> 00:03:52.255
Operations check.

45
00:03:52.255 --> 00:03:54.456
Airspace: Riyadh as above.

46
00:03:54.456 --> 00:03:56.423
Shipping: the Hormuz attack as above.

47
00:03:56.423 --> 00:04:06.296
Internet and telecom: Georgia Tech's internet outage measurements show Crimea's connectivity signal close to its usual level through the past day, with no outage alerts.

48
00:04:06.296 --> 00:04:10.591
That is a recovery in the measured signal, not proof of full service.

49
00:04:10.591 --> 00:04:14.503
Power: no verified update on Crimea's power supply.

50
00:04:14.503 --> 00:04:15.431
Borders: Uganda as above.

51
00:04:15.431 --> 00:04:17.835
Hazards: Cyclone Arnab as above.

52
00:04:17.835 --> 00:04:19.848
That's today's brief.

53
00:04:19.848 --> 00:04:21.245
Current as of five forty-five a.m.

54
00:04:21.245 --> 00:04:24.675
Central, Monday.

55
00:04:24.675 --> 00:04:27.127
Tueri summarizes public reporting.

56
00:04:27.127 --> 00:04:31.711
It is not legal, medical, or security advice for any specific situation.

57
00:04:31.711 --> 00:04:34.095
Check the original source before you act.

58
00:04:34.095 --> 00:04:42.973
Tueri Systems provides information verification, communications support, and operational decision support for organizations working in changing conditions.

59
00:04:42.973 --> 00:04:45.605
Learn more at tuerisystems.com.

60
00:04:45.605 --> 00:04:46.696
Thanks for listening.
